  "educational": {
    "scientificImportance": "Boston fern is a non-flowering vascular plant that reproduces via spores, representing the pteridophyte lineage that dominated terrestrial ecosystems 360 million years ago and lacks the seed-plant hormonal pathways (gibberellin fruit set, ethylene ripening) present in all other plants in this simulator.",
    "studentRelevance": "Comparing fern responses to those of seed plants highlights which stress mechanisms are ancient and conserved (ROS, antioxidants) versus which are angiosperm-specific (ethylene signaling, fruit ripening chemistry).",
    "indoorOutdoor": "both",
    "realWorldIssue": "Boston ferns are among the most effective indoor plants for absorbing formaldehyde and xylene from building materials, making them a living model for bioremediation of sick-building syndrome VOCs."
  },
